================================================================================
WITH THANKS — THE WORK THIS ONE IS BUILT ON
================================================================================

Two people wrote down the hardest parts before we got here, and published them.
This HAL would not exist without them.

Nothing was copied from either project — every file here was written fresh — but
the *shape* of a Windows NT 4.0 PowerPC HAL, and the Cuda/ADB handshake for this
exact silicon, were learned by reading their source.  That is a derivation, it is
why this project is GPL-2.0, and recording it properly is the least we owe them.

If you find this project useful, the two below are where the debt belongs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. entii-for-workcubes — the HAL this one learned its shape from
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The contract between the NT kernel and a PowerPC HAL is barely documented: the
DDK names the functions and says little about the order, the ownership or the
invariants.  halartx is a HAL that works, and reading it turned months of
guessing into an afternoon.

    Wack0 (Rairii)          https://github.com/Wack0/entii-for-workcubes
    License: GPL-2.0        commit c9b041da (2026-02-26), directory halartx/

The facts below came from reading it, and each file that used one says so in its
own header:

    src/irql.c      the IRQL-to-mask model, and that the HAL owns the IRQL in the PCR
    src/ints.c      external-interrupt dispatch through PCR->InterruptRoutine, and the
                    KINTERRUPT-from-dispatch-code trick
    src/clock.c     the decrementer reload, the KeUpdateSystemTime call, the
                    KeSetTimeIncrement use
    src/init.c      the phase 0/1 order and the PRCB version check
    src/dma.c       which adapter routines may be stubs (hwsup.c)
    src/misc.c      which ARC and kernel-debugger routines may be stubs
                    (arccalls.c, kd.c)
    src/thunk.S     the descriptor-call idea, and the shape of the cache loops (cache.s)
    src/vga.c       the NT OEM font's layout: the glyph Map[] and its column-major
    src/display.c   bytes (display.c)
    include/nt.h    DDK interface layouts, as reproduced in nt4/hal/{arc.h,hal.h,
                    halppc.h,ppcdef.h} and halartx/source/nthal.h

Also the intended future source of a keyboard/mouse port driver of our own:
`fpsidrv/`, a complete NT 4.0 PowerPC port driver with source.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. maciNTosh and maciNTosh-bandit — the Cuda/ADB handshake, for the same silicon
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Because this code exists, the Cuda protocol was never the unknown here.  Getting a
keyboard working still took five attempts, and not one of them failed on the
protocol; every failure was a mistake of our own.

Two projects, and it is worth being exact about who wrote what, because the file
we read is mostly the parent project's work:

    Wack0 (Rairii)          https://github.com/Wack0/maciNTosh
    License: GPL-2.0        NT for New World / Old World Power Macintosh

        The author of the Cuda code and of the HAL<->driver contract.
        inc/halpxi.h is byte-identical between the two projects, and of the 268
        substantive lines in the pxi.c we read, 208 — 95% — are identical to
        maciNTosh's own arcgrackle/source/pxi.c.

    MCJack123               https://github.com/MCJack123/maciNTosh-bandit
    License: GPL-2.0        commit bafef57, a fork of maciNTosh

        The reason any of it applied to a Network Server: the port to
        Bandit-class machines, which is what moves Cuda to Grand Central
        + 0x16000 — this machine's address, and the ~5% of the file that made
        it usable here.  Also the source that identified where ADB code for
        this chipset existed at all.

    src/cuda.c      the Cuda handshake follows arcbandit/source/pxi.c (and so,
                    at 95%, maciNTosh's arcgrackle/source/pxi.c): Grand Central's
                    VIA, TIP/ByteAck/TREQ over the shift register, the attention
                    byte to discard, TREQ-high meaning last byte.  The three ADB
                    entry points (HalPxiCommandAdb, HalPxiAdbSetCallback,
                    HalPxiAdbAutopoll) take their names and prototypes from
                    inc/halpxi.h — Wack0's file, unchanged in the fork — which is
                    the private HAL-to-driver contract maciNTosh's keyboard driver
                    expects.

Both projects' `adb_bus.c` / `adb_kbd.c` in turn carry:

    Open Hack'Ware BIOS ADB bus support, ported to OpenBIOS
    Copyright (c) 2005 Jocelyn Mayer
    Copyright (c) 2005 Stefan Reinauer
    GPL-2.0

which is named here for completeness; this project's `cuda.c` was written from
`pxi.c` and the hardware documentation, not from those files.  maciNTosh's own
acknowledgements credit baselibc, The Homebrew Channel, libogc, OpenBIOS,
coreboot, lib9660 and Petit FatFs for parts of its ARC firmware; this project
uses none of those components.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Binaries read but never redistributed
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The exact semantics of the three `HalPxi*` entry points were read out of
maciNTosh's own `halgoss.dll` and `usbadb.sys`, and the field offsets in
`include/nt.h` out of Microsoft's shipped `NTKRNLMP.EXE`, with `tools/pe-dis.py`.

Those binaries are NOT in this repository and must never be added:

  * maciNTosh's NT binaries (halgoss.dll, usbadb.sys, atapimio.sys, offrmbuf.sys)
    are GPL-2.0 with no published source.  Running one locally is unrestricted —
    the GPL governs distribution, not use — but this project could not accompany
    a copy with the corresponding source or a written offer, so GPL-2.0 section 3
    cannot be satisfied and redistribution is not something we may do.
  * Microsoft's binaries, headers and import libraries are proprietary.  Note that
    entii-for-workcubes vendors Microsoft's NT 4.0 DDK headers under `nt4/`; this
    project deliberately does not, and reads them only as a reproduction of the
    documented interface.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Everything else
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The rest — Grand Central interrupt dispatch, Bandit configuration space and bus
address translation, the Cirrus 54M30 console, the partition table, the ARC
environment, the ESCC console, the machine-check handler, the build tooling — is
original work of this project, written from hardware documentation (Apple,
Motorola, Symbios, Zilog, Cirrus), the observed ARC tree, and behaviour watched
under a debugger.

Nothing in this repository is derived from leaked Windows NT source code.

The full log, one row per borrowed thing with its origin, licence and extent, is
PROVENANCE.md.  The rules that keep it that way are CONTRIBUTING.md.

To Wack0 (Rairii) and MCJack123: thank you.  You published work you did not have
to publish, for machines almost nobody still runs, and it is the reason Windows NT
now boots on an Apple Network Server.  Wack0's name is on both halves of the debt:
the HAL this one learned its shape from, and the Cuda code it learned the keyboard
from.  MCJack123's is on the port that brought the second one within reach.
